const description = 'Practical strength training guides: 1RM testing protocols, percentage-based programming, peaking blocks, Wilks scores, and the research behind each.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Strength Training Guides</h1>
  <p>Plain-language guides on the topics our calculators support. Reviewed by the <a href="/editorial">editorial team</a> before publication. Each guide covers the practical application of strength science — not theory for its own sake, but the information you need to use the calculators effectively and make better training decisions.</p>

  <h2>Programming and testing</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/guides/how-to-test-1rm-safely">How to test your 1RM safely</a> — When to test, when not to test, the full warm-up scheme, attempt selection strategy, safety equipment requirements, and recovery after testing. For lifters considering direct 1RM testing rather than estimation from submaximal sets.</li>
    <li><a href="/guides/percentage-based-programming">Percentage-based programming</a> — How to use your estimated or tested 1RM to program training weeks and blocks. Percentage zones by training goal, weekly session structure, when to recalculate, and what to do when the prescribed percentages feel too easy or too hard.</li>
    <li><a href="/guides/peaking-block-strength">Peaking blocks for strength</a> — Structuring a 4–8 week peak to hit a new 1RM. Sample 6-week peaking template, intensity and volume progression, deload timing, common peaking mistakes, and meet-day attempt strategy for competitive lifters.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Scoring and comparison</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/guides/wilks-score-explained">Wilks score explained</a> — How the Wilks coefficient formula works, what scores mean across competitive levels, limitations of the original formula, and when to use Wilks versus newer systems like DOTS and IPF GL Points.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Related calculators</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/">One rep max calculator</a> — Estimate your 1RM from a submaximal set using four validated formulas.</li>
    <li><a href="/powerlifting-total-calculator">Powerlifting total calculator</a> — Combine your squat, bench, and deadlift into a competition total.</li>
    <li><a href="/wilks-calculator">Wilks calculator</a> — Normalize your total across bodyweights for cross-athlete comparison.</li>
    <li><a href="/macro-calculator">Macro calculator for strength athletes</a> — Set protein, carbohydrate, and fat targets for your training goal.</li>
  </ul>
</BaseLayout>
